      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait so if i use it for work but it's in my house it's just personal? i thought if i use a specific part of my home for business i can deduct the stuff in it. like if i buy a desk or a new monitor for my design work, can i write those off as business expenses since they are specifically for the studio?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, the IRS allows you to deduct certain business expenses from your taxable income if they're directly related to your freelance business. This includes expenses for furniture and office equipment used in a home office specifically for your business.\n\nTo deduct home office expenses, you must use a single, regular location in your home exclusively and regularly for conducting business. As long as you can clearly delineate your workspace and show that it is used solely for business purposes, those expenses can be reported as business deductions.\n\n\nHere are a few steps to take for a clear deduction:\n\n1. Set up a separate room, part of a room, or a distinct business space exclusively for business purposes.\n\n2. Use this space regularly throughout the year for your business-related activities.\n\n3. Keep records of the purchase, installation, and usage of any business-related items like your desk and monitor.\n\n4. Calculate the business use percentage of your home expenses (such as rent, utilities, and equipment like your chair, desk, or monitor). Your deductions for these items will be proportional to the percentage your home office takes up in your home.\n\n5. Be able to explain and justify your deductions to the IRS if asked.\n\nIn conclusion, your new desk or monitor, since they're deemed necessary for your design",
